  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA large expansive trough departs the region into early next week.\nBefore it does so, weak disturbances within the trough will provide\nchances for showers tonight and continued cloud cover into Sunday\nnight. Due to the cloud cover forecast, the diurnal temperature curve\nwas flattened from the baseline NBM, making the daytime highs a bit \ncooler today and Sunday and the nighttime lows slightly warmer.\nForecast soundings are very dry in the mid and upper levels tonight,\nbut a weak disturbance dropping south out of the Great Lakes may\nprovide enough forcing. Low-level lapse rates remain steep given the\nconstant cold air advection, supporting these low level showers. The\ncoverage of showers should be pretty scattered tonight, with most\nlocations dry to start Sunday morning.\n\nHigh pressure builds over the region Monday, helping to erode away\nany remaining moisture and low-level cloud cover by the afternoon.\nTemperatures climb back into the 50s, which is much closer to\nseasonal normals. The same high pressure shifts to the east of the\nregion midweek, supporting additional warming across the area. Above\nnormal temperatures then persist through the end of the week. \n\nA weak trough may move through the Great Lakes and upper Midwest on \nThursday, but at this time, conditions look too dry to allow for any \nrenewed chances of rainfall.",
